From: Christian Schaller <cschalle@redhat.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 12:41:56 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 0/3] media: i2c: imx471: selection, test pattern and gain fixes

Three fixes for the IMX471 driver, found while investigating poor image
quality from the front camera of a Lenovo ThinkPad X1 Carbon Gen 14,
where the sensor feeds an Intel IPU7 and all processing is done by
libcamera's software ISP.

Patch 1 makes V4L2_SEL_TGT_CROP work. The target currently ends its case
with break and so returns -EINVAL, and no crop rectangle is ever stored
in the subdev state, so the rectangle has to be described and stored as
well. libcamera reports this one itself ("The sensor kernel driver needs
to be fixed") and silently assumes readout covers the full pixel array,
which it does not.

Patch 2 fixes TEST_PATTERN_MODE being declared CCI_REG8 when 0x0600 is a
16 bit register, which left the test pattern generator inoperative.

Patch 3 raises IMX471_ANA_GAIN_MAX from 800 to 960. The gain law is
gain = 1024 / (1024 - code), so the current limit caps analogue gain at
4.57x where the sensor reaches 16x, giving up 1.8 stops. On this machine
the software ISP saturates exposure and analogue gain together and still
underexposes an ordinary indoor scene by roughly 3.8 stops.

All three were tested on the affected hardware, built into a Fedora
kernel package:

 - V4L2_SEL_TGT_CROP goes from failing with -EINVAL to returning
   (424,624)/3856x2176, and libcamera's four complaints about the crop
   rectangle disappear.
 - The test pattern generator produces colour bars where it previously
   produced nothing. The bar order also independently confirms the
   SRGGB Bayer order the driver advertises.
 - Analogue gain code 960 measures 16.07x against a predicted 16x, and
   3.48x over the previous ceiling against 3.50x predicted.

---
These patches were prepared with assistance from Claude (Anthropic).
All changes were reviewed and tested on the affected hardware.

